# Agent Instructions

This project is a .NET streaming extractor integration built on the Intropy
framework. One run = one batch: detect a complete batch of files on the
source (incoming prefix + ready marker), stream each file record-by-record in
constant memory through its registered streamed extractor, publish one
summary CloudEvent to a Dapr pub/sub topic, archive the consumed inputs, and
exit. Scheduling lives outside the block — activation cadence is deployment
configuration (a Kubernetes CronJob in production); locally the system host
runs the block once at startup.

## Project overview

- Framework: .NET 10
- App type: Run-to-completion console job (no web endpoints; one batch
  detection + processing cycle per run, then exit). Hosted by the framework's
  `RunToCompletionRunner`, which owns the Dapr sidecar lifecycle, tracing,
  and the exit-code contract: `0` success / nothing-ready / cancelled, `1`
  job failure, `2` infrastructure failure (sidecar unavailable).
- Runner: the framework's `RunToCompletionRunner` (in
  `Intropy.Framework.Hosting`), driven by `{{ .name }}.Batch.BatchJob` — an
  `IRunToCompletionJob` that adapts the sidecar-free `BatchPipeline` and
  reports a `JobRunSummary` (a completed batch = Processed 1; nothing ready =
  Empty; any failure = Failed 1).
- App id: `{{ .appId }}`
- Source port: `{{ .port }}` — the named interface the extractor reads
  the outside world through; the system host declares it in `Ports.cs`
  and the Dapr binding uses the same name, resolved to a local folder in
  development. In production the same name resolves to the environment's
  binding from the deploy overlay's GitOps values — rebinding
  the port is a deployment change, never a code change
- Pub/sub component: `pubsub`
- Topic: `{{ .topic }}` — the extractor's half of the system contract; the
  consuming component subscribes to the same topic
- CloudEvent envelope: source `{{ if .eventSource }}{{ .eventSource }}{{ else }}{{ .eventSourceValue }}{{ end }}`, type `{{ if .eventType }}{{ .eventType }}{{ else }}{{ .eventTypeValue }}{{ end }}`
- Idempotency Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .idempotencyAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
- Business Incident Service: reached via Dapr service invocation, app id
  `{{ .businessIncidentsAppId }}` (from `src/appsettings.json`, overridable per environment)
- Shared contracts: the published contract (`{{ .contract }}` — a batch
  summary: batch id + per-file record/reject counts, with a nested per-file
  record) lives in the sibling project `../{{ .contracts }}` (namespace
  `{{ .contracts }}`), referenced from `src/{{ .name }}.csproj`. The sibling
  is scaffolded by this template's dependency declaration; when it already
  exists (another component created it) the scaffold leaves it untouched.
  The batch model (`{{ .contract }}Batch`) and the per-record shapes
  (`Source{{ .contract }}Record`, `{{ .contract }}Record`) stay local in
  `src/Model/`.

## Important files

- `Taskfile.yml` - build and test tasks (`task build`, `task test`; the
  integration suite also runs separately via `task test:integration`).
- `Dockerfile` - multi-stage image build on the .NET chiseled runtime
  (`docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .` —
  the shared contracts sibling enters as a named context); the image contains
  only the app — the Dapr sidecar and components come from the deployment
  environment.
- `src/Program.cs` - entrypoint: builds the service provider via `Composition`
  and runs the framework `RunToCompletionRunner`.
- `src/Composition/Composition.cs` - every service registration: the keyed
  source adapter (`IFileStreamAdapter`), the streamed extractors (one
  `AddStreamedExtractor` per entity type), the publish edge (the sender is a
  DI-registered `SendStep<TCtx>` — a `DaprTopicPublisher` in production), and
  the batch pipeline (`AddBatchPipeline`). `BuildBatchPipeline(builder, sp)`
  is public so the integration suite builds the pipeline exactly as
  production composition does. Note the namespace/type collision:
  `{{ .name }}.Composition` is both namespace and class, so call sites inside
  nested namespaces use the fully qualified `Composition.Composition.*`.
- `src/Batch/` - the batch chain: `BatchJob` (runner adapter), `DetectStep`
  (component-owned detection: incoming prefix + ready marker; also the batch
  idempotency seam — an archived batch must be invisible to it),
  `SubPipelineStep` (matches files to streamed extractors by entity type,
  builds each file's `StreamSource` from the adapter, accumulates per-file
  counts), `SerializeSummaryStep` (one CloudEvent per batch; subject = batch
  id), `ArchiveStep` (moves consumed inputs + marker to the processed prefix).
- `src/Batch/{{ .contract }}BatchContext.cs` - the batch chain's context:
  carries the detected batch and one `StreamedContext` per processed file.
- `src/Process/Streamed/` - the sample streamed extractor's steps
  (`Deserializer` locates the record array in the source envelope;
  `{{ .contract }}ProcessStep` is the fused validate+transform). Duplicate
  per entity type.
- `src/Constants.cs` - component/topic names, keyed-adapter key, CloudEvent
  envelope values, the sample entity type (root namespace — consumed by both
  `src` and the tests).
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Integration/` - batch-run and composition tests
  against `Intropy.Framework.Testing` fakes (`task test:integration`;
  `task coverage` reports branch coverage).

## Configuration

Settings live in `src/appsettings.json` (copied to the build output),
overridden by `appsettings.{Environment}.json` and environment variables —
`Composition` builds `IConfiguration` from the binary directory, registers it
in DI, and reads the platform-service app ids from `PlatformServices:*`.
Batch layout (incoming/output/processed prefixes + ready-marker name) binds
to `src/Configuration/IngestOptions.cs` from the `Ingest` section.
Deployment overlays the file via ConfigMap.

## Build and test

Components do not run standalone — this extractor runs via its system host,
which runs it once at startup and provides every Dapr component (source
binding, pub/sub, platform services). A re-run is a host restart. The
component-level loop is build and test:

```bash
task build
task test
```

The integration suite builds the pipeline exactly as production composition
does, fakes only the edges (`InMemoryFileAdapter` — which implements the
streaming `IFileStreamAdapter` contract — `FakeTopic`,
`FakeIdempotencyServiceClient`, `FakeBusinessIncidentServiceClient` — from
`Intropy.Framework.Testing`), and asserts on fake state. `task coverage`
runs the integration suite with branch coverage (Cobertura report under the
integration project's `TestResults/`).

## Development notes

- The source adapter is registered as a keyed singleton
  (`Constants.SourceFileAdapterKey`) against `IFileStreamAdapter` — the
  streaming extension of `IFileAdapter` (open read/write streams, exists,
  move). Inject it via `[FromKeyedServices(...)]` or resolve via
  `GetRequiredKeyedService<IFileStreamAdapter>(...)`. The default
  registration is the zero-dependency `LocalFileStreamAdapter`; production
  swaps in the SDK adapter (e.g. `Intropy.Framework.Adapters.Azure`'s
  `AzureBlobStorageStreamAdapter`) at this one registration — credentials
  from configuration, never hard-coded.
- Batch idempotency is the detect step's contract, not a framework step: an
  archived batch is invisible to detection, so a completed batch never
  re-runs. Keep the summary event's subject/id stable (the batch id) so
  downstream consumers can deduplicate a re-delivery after a mid-chain crash.
- One streamed extractor per entity type: duplicate the
  `AddStreamedExtractor` registration and the `src/Process/Streamed/` steps
  per entity. `SubPipelineStep` fails the batch when a file's entity type has
  no registered extractor — partial batches are never published.
- A technical failure (dead source, dead broker, archive move failure) fails
  the run with exit 1 and leaves the batch in place for the next scheduled
  run; a ready-but-incomplete batch is a business failure routed to the
  Business Incident Service, keyed on the batch id.
{{- if .empty }}
- Detection, serialize, and the streamed step bodies are scaffolded empty
  (`NotImplementedException`). The batch chain wiring (sub-pipeline
  resolution, sender, archive, business-incident routing) stays in place.
  When you implement: detection defines the producer's delivery format
  (folder naming, expected files, ready marker); keep the batch id stable
  across retries.
- The integration suite is real and fails out of the box by design — `task
  test` is red until the steps land (only `CompositionTests` starts green).
  The pinned behaviors flip green one by one as the implementation
  progresses; no test edits are needed to get there.
{{- end }}

